@H@T0INDENT is a (re)formater for C source textsSyntax: indent [<opts>] [<inpath> [<outpath>]] [<opts>]Function: INDENT reads the C source text file given by <inpath>, reformats it           and writes it to <outpath>. If <inpath> is not given INDENT reads          <stdin> and writes to <stdout>. If <outpath> is not given and the          '-st' option is not set INDENT creates a backup file and uses          <inpath> as the output file. If the '-st' option is set INDENT writes          to <stdout>.Options:    -?          print usage            -<format>   format options            -npro       do not read '.indent.pro'            -st         write to standard output            -v          verbose mode on            -nv         verbose mode off (default)@D1 UFormat_Options 2Profile_File 3Specials 4Edition_Notes 5@T2A preceeding 'n' switches the option off.  -[n]bacc    blanklines around conditional compilation (default: on)  -[n]badp    blanklines after declarations of the first block (def.: on)   -[n]bad     blanklines after declarations (default: on)   -[n]bap     blanklines after procs (default: on)   -[n]bbb     blanklines before block commants (default: on)   -[n]bc      never break declarations after commas (default: on)   -bl         brace should not be on same line as if, while etc. (def.: on)   -br         brace should be on same line as if, while etc. (def.: on)   -[n]bs      blank after sizeof (default: on)   -[n]cdb     comment delimiter on blankline (default: on)   -cd[=]<num> set column in which comment after declarations should be               put (default: 0)   -[n]ce      else should cuddle up to '}' (default: on)   -ci[=]<num> set indentiation between the edge of code and               continuation lines (default: 0)   -cli[=]<num>   set case label indent level (default: 0)   -c[=]<num>  set column in which comments to the right of code should               start (default: 33)   -di[=]<num> set column to indent declared identifiers to (def.: 16)   -[n]dj      declarations should be left justified (default: off)   -d[=]<num>  comments not to the right of of code will be placed <num>               indentation levels to the left of code (default: 0)   -[n]eei     continuation lines from the expression part of 'if(e)',               'while(e)', 'for(e;e;e)' should be indented an extra tab               stop (default: on)   -[n]ei      'else if' pairs are handled specially (default: on)   -[n]fc1     comment which start in column 1 are to be magically               reformated (default: on)   -[n]ip      indent parameters (default: on)   -i[=]<num>  set size of one indentation level (default: 8)   -lc[=]<num> set maximum line lenght for comments (def.: '-l' - value)   -[n]lp      continued code whithin parentheses will be lined up to the               open parenthesis (default: on)   -l[=]<num>  set maximum line lenth (default: 78)   -[n]pcs     no space after fucntion name (default: off)   -[n]psl     newline between type of the function and its name (def.: on)   -[n]ps      pointer as binop (default: on)   -[n]sc      comment continuation lines should have a star (def.: on)   -[n]sob     swallow optional blanklines (default: on)   -[n]tab     use TAB-characters where possible (default: on)   -troff      generate input for 'troff' (default: off)               fonts for 'troff':   -fbc[=]<font>     block comment font (default: 'I2')   -fbx[=]<font>     box comment font (default: 'I2')   -fb[=]<font>      major body font (default: 'R'   -fc[=]<font>      same line comment font (default: 'I')   -fk[=]<font>      keyword font (default: 'B')   -fs[=]<font>      string font (default: 'L')   -T[=]<str>  add <str> as an declaration keyword to the keyword table @T3A list of format options reflecting the own default style may be stored ina file named '.indent.pro'. This file is searched for in '/dd/sys', the HOMEdirectory and the current directory.All found files are handled.@T4INDENT can be controlled by the comments '/* INDENT ON */' and '/* INDENTOFF */' in the C source text. A part of a C source enclosed with '/* INDENT OFF */' and '/* INDENT ON */'is not processed by INDENT but simply written as it is.@T5The source of INDENT was originally written by University of California,Berkeley, University of Illinois, Urbana and Sun Microsystems, Inc.It was taken from MINIX.INDENT has been adapted to OS-9/68k by         Hubert Nehring        Dahlemer Str. 4        D-W-5090 Leverkusen 1The adaption has been carried out by:-   changing the option parsing method in order to be compatible with the    OS-9 option convention-   adding a -? option-   adding the -[n]tabs option-   using register variables extensively in order to minimize the size of the    resulting program and to maximize its execution speed.    It is unknown (to the adaptor) if the 'troff' option is working correctly.